wn very clearly is that for the world  to keep temperatures at 1.5 degrees  celsius by the end of the century  something they pledged to do in the  paris agreement six years ago  then the world needs to level out its  co2 emissions by 2050 and its greenhouse  gas emissions by 2070.  now modi's pledged to sort out the first  the  half of that about greenhouse gases by  2070 what he hasn't been clear on is  what that means for carbon dioxide  emissions by 2050 whether that would  also be the case  if so it would mean that his targets are  consistent with the paris agreement what  we're also hearing though is that the  targets for 2030 that we just heard are  much more important if india does really  manage to build out that much renewable  energy to cut its co2 emissions then  that will put it in good stead to  actually continue follow through with  this and  turn its economy which is very heavily  co-dependent into one that's actually  climate friendly  right and you might have noticed i just  said earlier alleged commitments because  world leaders have promised many things  at these climate change conferences over  the years and failed to deliver so how  reliable are the pledges that have been  made  so that's the thing about these climate  conferences these are promises these are  pledges and they have been broken before  most notably in 2009 at the copenhagen  summit rich countries promised promised  poorer ones 100 billion us dollars a  year in climate finance to help them  green their economies to help them adapt  to the effects of climate change  but that never came through last year  they did not meet that target and now  we're seeing we're going to have  negotiations all of today to kind of  haggle over that sum of money to come up  with a credible plan to fulfill that  promise belatedly  and so pledges are one thing and it's  why climate scientists that we speak to  are consistently stressing  countries need to do things now they  need to act immediately to 