d states and  germany  but with fossil fuels now clearly  identified as the driver of disastrous  climate change the question now is  whether india will act in time  or leave the problem to the next  generation  i mean what you see is tension between  emerging economies and developed  countries  who needs to do more  so  the developed economies have  been  emitting fossil emit burning fossil  fuels and emitting co2 historically that  have caused the majority of this problem  what people don't necessarily realize is  that even though big countries like  china and india are big emitters today  there's still a historical carbon debt  that rich countries in theory would pay  poorer countries in order to kind of  equal things out now the paris climate  agreement addresses this partly rich  countries signed up to have these common  but differentiated responsibilities  and if they were to actually deliver on  this it would mean acknowledging that  they would have to have more ambitious  climate targets  and  cut their fossil fuel usage earlier and  that's what a lot of poorer countries  are calling for at this conference  what's unclear though is what that means  for the poorer countries themselves many  of which are burning fossil fuels like  coal as we just heard which have a huge  negative which really hurt their own  citizens it causes huge problems for  mortality causes lung cancer and so on  and so there's an interest at home  domestically to actually sort this  problem out as well even beyond what  we're hearing on the international stage  here  right and of course the key point is of  course mother nature doesn't keep score  it doesn't really matter what the  historical past  you know determined in terms of the  developed country's responsibilities we  have a climate emergency ajit naranjan  thank you so much for joining us  thank you very much  you
 

